title: Literary Techniques
subject: English
level: GCSE
emoji: 🎭
front-template: Define: {}
description: Name the technique – and explain its effect.
---
Alliteration :: Repetition of the same initial consonant sound in nearby words: 'peter piper picked'.
Assonance :: Repetition of vowel sounds in nearby words: 'the rain in Spain'.
Onomatopoeia :: A word that imitates the sound it describes: 'crash', 'buzz'.
Simile :: Compares two things using 'like' or 'as': 'brave as a lion'.
Metaphor :: States that one thing IS another: 'the world is a stage'.
Personification :: Giving human qualities to a non-human thing: 'the wind whispered'.
Hyperbole :: Deliberate exaggeration for effect: 'I've told you a million times'.
Oxymoron :: Two contradictory words together: 'bittersweet', 'deafening silence'.
Pathetic fallacy :: Weather or nature reflecting the mood: 'storm clouds gathered as he grieved'.
Juxtaposition :: Placing two contrasting things side by side to highlight differences.
Foreshadowing :: Hints at events that will happen later in the story.
Irony :: A contrast between expectation and reality.
Dramatic irony :: When the audience knows something the characters do not.
Imagery :: Language that creates a picture or sensory experience in the reader's mind.
Symbolism :: Using an object, colour or image to represent a bigger idea.
Motif :: A recurring image, idea or symbol that develops a theme.
Allusion :: An indirect reference to another text, person, or event.
Euphemism :: A mild expression used in place of something harsh: 'passed away'.
Rhetorical question :: A question asked for effect, not expecting an answer.
Anaphora :: Repeating a word or phrase at the start of successive clauses.
Tricolon (rule of three) :: A list of three words or phrases for emphasis and rhythm.
Soliloquy :: A speech in which a character speaks their thoughts aloud, alone on stage.
Enjambment :: A sentence running over a line break in poetry without punctuation.
Caesura :: A pause in the middle of a line of poetry, often shown by punctuation.
